WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.461
Lesson 1.1: Composing an Original Song with GenAI.

2
00:00:05.461 --> 00:00:08.519
GENAI 101 - Module 1: Music.

3
00:00:08.519 --> 00:00:15.072
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:15.672 --> 00:00:18.872
In this lesson you'll compose an original song.

5
00:00:18.872 --> 00:00:30.784
You'll write the lyrics yourself, two verses and a chorus,
then write a style prompt, generate three versions of the
song, and choose the strongest one using musical criteria.

6
00:00:30.784 --> 00:00:38.748
At the end you'll share it in the gallery with a short
rationale explaining which prompt choices made the
difference.

7
00:00:39.348 --> 00:00:40.915
Here's the core idea.

8
00:00:40.915 --> 00:00:45.167
A text-to-song tool takes a style prompt and your lyrics.

9
00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:54.194
If the prompt is vague, like 'a nice pop song', the model
makes every musical decision for you and the result is
generic.

10
00:00:54.194 --> 00:01:11.800
If the prompt is specific, like 'upbeat indie pop at one
hundred eighteen beats per minute, bright acoustic guitar
and handclaps, warm female alto, sparse first verse building
to a full chorus', the model has constraints to work within.

11
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.337
Constraints are where style lives.

12
00:01:14.937 --> 00:01:20.609
Use the same five-part frame for every music prompt in this
course.

13
00:01:20.609 --> 00:01:24.843
Genre and era: what tradition does this belong to?

14
00:01:24.843 --> 00:01:29.330
Tempo and feel: how fast is it, and how does it move?

15
00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:32.885
Instrumentation: what do we actually hear?

16
00:01:32.885 --> 00:01:35.087
Vocal: who sings, and how?

17
00:01:35.087 --> 00:01:41.606
And arc and mood: how does the song change over time, and
how should it feel?

18
00:01:41.606 --> 00:01:50.157
Think of these as the six decisions a producer makes, minus
the key, which most tools choose for you.

19
00:01:50.757 --> 00:01:59.120
Your lyrics need structure tags: verse one, chorus, verse
two, bridge, and so on, in square brackets.

20
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.847
The tags tell the model where sections begin.

21
00:02:02.847 --> 00:02:08.395
Without them, choruses land in odd places and the form falls
apart.

22
00:02:08.395 --> 00:02:13.778
This is your first taste of computational thinking in the
course.

23
00:02:13.778 --> 00:02:18.332
You are sequencing the form so a machine can follow it.

24
00:02:18.932 --> 00:02:20.450
Generation is cheap.

25
00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:21.664
Judgment is not.

26
00:02:21.664 --> 00:02:27.660
When you regenerate, change one variable at a time so you
can hear what it did.

27
00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:36.463
A good order is: get the form right first, then the tempo
and feel, then the instrumentation, and finally the vocal.

28
00:02:36.463 --> 00:02:53.613
Score every version on the evaluation grid in the lesson
page: does the form follow your tags, does the tempo match
the brief, are the lyrics sung as written, is the vocal
phrasing natural, and would you play this for a class?

29
00:02:53.613 --> 00:03:01.885
When you've chosen, post it in the gallery with your lyrics,
your prompt, your rationale, and your AI credit.

30
00:03:01.885 --> 00:03:03.175
Have fun with it.
